YAAP -- Link the Male Human Lawful Knight

I finally decided to play a class other than Monks, and it turns out
that I like Knights more than I thought I would.  The Knight pets are
kind of like a Bag of Holding and Speed Boots stuck together.  And
zapping a warhorse with a speed monster wand, and then kicking it,
resulted in the warhorse going ridiculously fast (I wasn't fully
paying attention, but I think a kicked warhorse goes faster if it was
previously zapped with a wand of speed monster).  I also didn't need
any projectile weapons, as the whole #jump + riding a pet + lance
combo took care of the difficult monsters.  And Gehennom was easier
than it usually is, as double damage Magic Missiles basically slaugter
everything down there.  Also, I'm so used to having automatic
searching (I'm usually playing Monks), that my Knight kept Excalibur
for the entire game.

However, the whole code of conduct thing was a bit annoying, as I
often had to kick a wall (or a door) to wake monsters up so I could
hit them.  I eventually got the Aggravate Monster intrinsic, which
lowered the amount of wall kicking I had to do.  And I wasn't too
thrilled that I couldn't write Elebereth, and then hit monsters while
they are fleeing.  And I wasn't too thrilled that I needed a pet to
kill the 'h' monsters in the Gnomish Mines, as just about all of them
were peaceful to a Knight.  And since this is my first Lawful
character, I really wasn't mentally prepared for the fact that the
mysterious force sometimes kicks me down 3 levels at a time.

This game did not want to give the Knight anything useful.  The Knight
ended up wishing for a polymorph wand, and polypiling mass amounts of
everything, as he was missing so many things for his ascension kit
that the wand of wishing didn't have enough wishes to get all the
items I needed.  Fortunately, the polypiling did give me way too many
potions-that-can-be-alchemized-into-full healing-potions, and it did
give me a lot of teleportation wands (which are great for the Astral
Plane).  I did find a second wand of wishing near the bottom of
Gehennom, but by the time I got that wand, I already had everything I
really needed.  So I used the second wand to wish for junk like extra
amulets of life saving, and an extra wand of death.

The only thing that really bothered me about the game is that after I
beat it, I found out that I wasn't at max luck.  In the middle of the
game, I maxed my luck by throwing identified gems at a white unicorn,
but I somehow lost the extra luck later in the game.  I think I
definitely lost luck when I ate the human corpse to get the aggravate
monster intrinsic, and I think I definitely lost luck when prayed on a
cross-aligned altar to get unholy water.  I wasn't paying attention
enough to the game to realize at the time that I was doing things that
decreased luck, and I should have thrown more identified gems at a
white unicorn after I did those things.



